1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,536
This is LT-Sentinel — a sidecar that gives Veea's Lobster Trap something it doesn't have today: memory across events.

2
00:00:08,036 --> 00:00:14,984
Lobster Trap is excellent at single-event deep prompt inspection — sub-millisecond regex, eight policy actions.

3
00:00:14,984 --> 00:00:17,738
But by design it is stateless across events.

4
00:00:17,738 --> 00:00:22,621
So it can't see three attack patterns that matter most in multi-agent systems.

5
00:00:22,621 --> 00:00:30,320
First, slow injection: an attacker drips pieces of 'ignore your rules' across many turns, and each turn alone looks benign.

6
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:36,893
Second, trust-then-burst: an agent behaves perfectly for thirty turns, then makes one extraction attempt.

7
00:00:36,893 --> 00:00:43,215
Third, tool-result poisoning: each tainted tool return looks like noise, but the pattern accumulates.

8
00:00:43,215 --> 00:00:46,220
LT-Sentinel is the layer that catches all three.

9
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:54,008
Three Lobster Trap instances run in parallel, each loaded with one of three policies — Trust, Observe, and Lockdown.

10
00:00:54,008 --> 00:00:55,202
They never restart.

11
00:00:55,202 --> 00:01:01,987
Sentinel adds a reverse proxy on port 8080 that routes to whichever instance matches the current trust tier.

12
00:01:01,987 --> 00:01:10,972
It tails all three audit logs, computes a per-agent error rate over a thirty-event window, and feeds it through two statistical control charts.

13
00:01:10,972 --> 00:01:17,569
When the resulting trust score crosses a threshold, Sentinel atomically swings the proxy to the new tier.

14
00:01:17,569 --> 00:01:19,768
No restart, no source modification.

15
00:01:20,268 --> 00:01:23,039
Every knob is anchored to literature.

16
00:01:23,039 --> 00:01:27,308
The smoothing factor comes from Lucas and Saccucci, 1990.

17
00:01:27,308 --> 00:01:32,849
The change-detection bounds from Page, 1954, and Hawkins and Olwell, 1998.

18
00:01:32,849 --> 00:01:35,995
The window size from Munz and Carle, 2008.

19
00:01:35,995 --> 00:01:40,338
The baseline prior from Agent Security Bench at ICLR 2025.

20
00:01:40,338 --> 00:01:42,660
Nothing here is a magic number.

21
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:49,589
On startup, three Lobster Trap instances come up — Trust, Observe, and Lockdown — alongside the Sentinel proxy.

22
00:01:49,589 --> 00:01:57,641
Notice the very first line: this is a restart — Sentinel rebuilt its state from an earlier run's audit log and resumed at the correct tier.

23
00:01:57,641 --> 00:02:02,622
That is why the startup clock here is later than the demo data you'll see in a moment.

24
00:02:02,622 --> 00:02:07,256
A crash in the middle of lockdown comes back up locked down — not at full trust.

25
00:02:07,756 --> 00:02:12,503
The demo drives all three attacks through a real LangGraph multi-agent system.

26
00:02:12,503 --> 00:02:15,607
The warm-up turns pass clean — every agent allowed.

27
00:02:15,607 --> 00:02:23,215
Then Scenario A, slow injection: the router keeps classifying the payloads, and the prompt-injection rules start firing DENY.

28
00:02:23,215 --> 00:02:26,380
Through the recovery phase, benign turns flow again.

29
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:39,799
Scenario B, trust-then-burst: fifteen clean H-R turns, then a four-request burst — the first blocked by Lobster Trap itself, the next three progressively riskier and caught by Sentinel's tiers.

30
00:02:39,799 --> 00:02:49,104
Scenario C, tool poisoning: eight tool returns escalate, hitting data-exfiltration, obfuscation, P-I-I, and prompt-injection rules in turn.

31
00:02:49,604 --> 00:02:51,008
Four agents, one chart.

32
00:02:51,008 --> 00:02:54,670
The green band is Trust, yellow is Observe, red is Lockdown.

33
00:02:54,670 --> 00:02:59,613
Finance stays pinned at one — it's the control, it never receives attack traffic.

34
00:02:59,613 --> 00:03:01,749
The router is the agent that moves.

35
00:03:01,749 --> 00:03:15,054
And here is the real finding: the router is the universal entry point — every user turn passes through it for classification first, so it accumulates exposure from all three scenarios and crosses every threshold first.

36
00:03:15,054 --> 00:03:20,425
Watch the middle of the chart — the router climbs back up, lockdown to observe to trust.

37
00:03:20,425 --> 00:03:27,932
That's the control chart decaying: once the attack window passes, clean traffic pulls the score back, with no manual reset.

38
00:03:28,432 --> 00:03:32,233
This is what Veea's brief called audit trails a regulator could read.

39
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:40,441
Each tier transition records the trigger agent — who tipped the system over — their trust score at the moment of crossing, and which threshold broke.

40
00:03:40,441 --> 00:03:45,840
It captures a full snapshot of every other agent's score, so you can prove they were not involved.

41
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:53,001
It lists the violating request IDs as evidence, and each one chains back through the events log into Lobster Trap's own audit log.

42
00:03:53,001 --> 00:03:58,014
It records the exact policy file that became active, and a one-line human-readable summary.

43
00:03:58,014 --> 00:04:00,328
Recoveries are logged with the same shape.

44
00:04:00,828 --> 00:04:14,221
Three hand-written policy files, a LangGraph multi-agent system, the Sentinel runtime, thirty-nine unit tests, two hundred and twenty calibration chains — everything in one open-source repo, on top of open-source Lobster Trap.

45
00:04:14,221 --> 00:04:23,643
Two resilience details worth a line: a dormant agent does not stay locked down forever — its trust score decays back toward baseline on a ten-minute half-life.

46
00:04:23,643 --> 00:04:27,376
And Sentinel rebuilds full state from its audit log on restart.

47
00:04:27,376 --> 00:04:32,947
Per-identity policy is the obvious next step, but it needs an upstream change in Lobster Trap.

48
00:04:32,947 --> 00:04:37,332
Thanks to Veea for open-sourcing the floor, and to lablab for the ceiling.

